---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { guides } from '../../data/guides';

const guide = guides.find((g) => g.slug === 'phone-fell-in-water-first-10-minutes')!;
const url = `https://fixthespeaker.com/guides/${guide.slug}`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Can I turn my phone on immediately to see if it works?',
    a: 'No. If water reached internal circuits, powering up the system sends current through wet traces, triggering shorts that destroy components that were otherwise salvageable. Wait until the phone has thoroughly dried.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does wireless charging work if the USB port is wet?',
    a: 'While wireless charging bypasses the physical port, make sure the back glass and charging coils are completely dry before placing the phone on a pad. Charging creates warmth, which can cause internal moisture to vaporize and condense on camera sensors.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long should I let a wet phone dry before turning it on?',
    a: 'Manufacturers recommend allowing at least 5 to 24 hours of drying in an area with good air circulation before powering on or connecting a charging cable.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is salt water more dangerous than fresh water?',
    a: 'Yes, significantly. Salt water is highly conductive and corrosive. If dropped in salt water, rinse the phone gently with fresh tap water immediately to wash away salt before drying, as dried salt crystals permanently corrode metal connections.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'Article',
      '@id': `${url}#article`,
      headline: guide.title,
      description: guide.description,
      datePublished: guide.published,
      dateModified: guide.updated,
      mainEntityOfPage: url,
      author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Dev', url: 'https://fixthespeaker.com/about' },
      publisher: { '@id': 'https://fixthespeaker.com/#organization' },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      '@id': `${url}#breadcrumb`,
      itemListElement: [
        {
          '@type': 'ListItem',
          position: 1,
          name: 'Fix The Speaker',
          item: 'https://fixthespeaker.com/',
        },
        {
          '@type': 'ListItem',
          position: 2,
          name: 'Guides',
          item: 'https://fixthespeaker.com/guides',
        },
        {
          '@type': 'ListItem',
          position: 3,
          name: guide.title,
          item: url,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      '@id': `${url}#faq`,
      mainEntity: faqs.map(({ q, a }) => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: q,
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: a,
        },
      })),
    },
  ],
};
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${guide.title} | Fix The Speaker`}
  description={guide.description}
  heading={guide.title}
  eyebrow="Guide"
  updated={guide.updated}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>
    The first 10 minutes after your phone falls in water determine whether it survives intact or suffers permanent
    electrical short-circuits. Retrieve the device immediately, power it off if submerged, disconnect all cables,
    and wipe the exterior dry with a clean cloth. Do not plug it into a charger or bury it in rice, as both actions
    accelerate short-circuits and port contamination.
  </p>
  <p>
    Water damage happens because dissolved minerals in water conduct electricity, bridging pins on circuit boards
    and causing instant short circuits or rapid electrochemical corrosion.
  </p>

  <h2>Quick fix steps</h2>
  <p>Act quickly and execute these seven steps within the first ten minutes:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Pull the phone out of the liquid immediately.</strong> Every second submerged increases the static
      water pressure against internal rubber gaskets and acoustic mesh seals.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Power down the phone immediately.</strong> If the phone was submerged, shut it off completely.
      Turning the phone off stops active electrical current from circulating through wet circuits, preventing
      electrolysis and solder shorts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Strip off the case, accessories, and SIM tray.</strong> Remove external cases and bumpers immediately.
      Cases trap liquid against seams and speaker grilles. If your device has a removable SIM tray, eject it and
      pat the tray dry.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dry the exterior thoroughly with a lint-free cloth.</strong> Use a clean microfiber towel to dry the
      screen, rear glass, and frame. Carefully dab around speaker openings and the charging port to absorb pooled
      surface liquid.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tap the phone gently to expel water from ports.</strong> Hold the device vertically with the charging
      port and speaker grilles facing downward. As
      <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/102643" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Apple Support recommends</a>,
      gently tap the phone against the heel of your hand to shake loose water drops held by surface tension.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rinse with fresh tap water if dropped in salt or pool water.</strong> Both
      <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108039" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Apple</a> and
      <a href="https://www.samsung.com/us/support/answer/ANS00077611/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Samsung</a>
      state that if your phone contacts non-fresh liquids (sea water, pool water, soda), gently rinse the affected
      area with fresh tap water, then wipe it dry. Salt and chemicals form corrosive crystals as they dry.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Position the device under cool, moving air.</strong> Prop your phone upright with the ports facing
      down in front of a household desk fan blowing cool air across the openings.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <figure class="my-8">
    <img
      src="/images/guides/phone-water-first-10-minutes-timeline.svg"
      alt="Emergency action timeline for the first 10 minutes after a phone falls in water: power off immediately, remove case and SIM, gently tap port downward, and place in cool moving air."
      class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-hairline bg-elevated"
      width="720"
      height="360"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <figcaption class="mt-2.5 text-center text-xs text-mute font-mono">
      Emergency protocol: 4-stage action plan for the first 10 minutes following water contact
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Why it happens</h2>
  <p>
    Modern flagship smartphones carry water resistance ratings certified under
    <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108039" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IEC standard 60529 (IP67 or IP68)</a>.
    These ratings mean the phone is engineered to resist freshwater immersion at specified depths (usually 1.5 to 6
    meters) for up to 30 minutes in static lab conditions. However:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Water resistance is not waterproof:</strong> Resistance relies on precision rubber O-rings,
      pressure-sensitive adhesive gaskets around the display, and hydrophobic mesh over microphones and speakers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Seals degrade over time:</strong> Normal wear, pocket friction, drops, and temperature fluctuations
      degrade waterproof adhesives. A phone dropped six months after purchase has significantly weaker resistance
      than a brand-new unit.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Electrochemical corrosion occurs rapidly:</strong> Pure water conducts electricity poorly, but tap,
      pool, and ocean water are full of dissolved ions. When voltage passes through ion-rich water, metal traces on
      circuit boards dissolve within minutes through electrolysis.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If your speaker sounds muffled once the phone is dry, review our dedicated guide on
    <a href="/guides/iphone-speaker-muffled-after-water">how to fix an iPhone speaker muffled after water</a> or use
    our <a href="/fix-iphone-speaker">Fix iPhone Speaker tool</a> to expel water from the grille.
  </p>

  <figure class="my-8">
    <img
      src="/images/guides/phone-protective-seals-diagram.svg"
      alt="Cross-section diagram of smartphone edge seals showing display adhesive, silicone port gasket, and hydrophobic speaker mesh."
      class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-hairline bg-elevated"
      width="720"
      height="360"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <figcaption class="mt-2.5 text-center text-xs text-mute font-mono">
      Chassis seal diagram: perimeter display adhesive, port gasket, and hydrophobic speaker mesh
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Critical mistakes to avoid in the first 10 minutes</h2>
  <p>What you avoid doing is just as important as what you do:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not put your phone in rice.</strong> Apple, Samsung, and Google all advise against rice. Rice
      absorbs water far slower than open air, blocks ventilation, and sheds starch powder into damp ports, creating
      a gummy paste.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not use a hairdryer, heat gun, or oven.</strong> Heat warps internal water-resistant adhesives and
      causes lithium-ion batteries to overheat or swell.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not plug in the charging cable.</strong> Connecting a charger sends voltage through damp connector
      pins, causing instant corrosion and burning out charging circuits.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not shake the phone violently.</strong> Vigorous shaking forces pooled water deeper into internal
      cavities past speaker membranes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not poke swabs or toothpicks into ports.</strong> Cotton swabs shed damp fibers inside ports, and
      sharp items can bend charging pins.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When it's real damage (and when to get repairs)</h2>
  <p>
    Even with prompt action, liquid may breach internal seals. Seek authorized professional service if you observe:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The phone refuses to power on or boot-loops.</strong> A device that cannot boot or restarts
      spontaneously indicates a shorted power management chip or damaged battery connection.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Display abnormalities.</strong> Green lines, flickering, dark patches, or condensation behind the
      glass mean water breached the display panel assembly.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Persistent liquid detection alerts.</strong> If the charging alert remains on after 24 to 48 hours of
      fan drying, internal charging contacts have corroded.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Unusual heat generation.</strong> If the phone feels warm while powered off, disconnect it from
      accessories and place it on a fire-safe surface immediately.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Manufacturer warranties explicitly exclude liquid damage. If repairs are necessary, technicians inspect internal
    Liquid Contact Indicators (LCIs) to determine out-of-warranty replacement options.
  </p>
  <p>
    To learn more about the science of clearing water from speakers using low-frequency acoustics, see our analysis
    on <a href="/guides/does-water-eject-sound-really-work">does water eject sound really work</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How we made this guide</h2>
  <p>
    This guide is based on official support pages from Apple, Samsung, and Google, linked above. We have not tested
    every phone model ourselves. If a step here ever conflicts with your manufacturer's own instructions, follow the
    manufacturer.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {faqs.map(({ q, a }) => (
    <>
      <h3>{q}</h3>
      <p>{a}</p>
    </>
  ))}

